  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-8,-15).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to two decimal places where necessary.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+(-15)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+225} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{289} \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{-15}{-8})| \\\\  & \u22481.080\\,84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the third quadrant, we add the reference angle to $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.080\\,84 \\\\  & \u22484.22,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(17,4.22).$",
